# Executive brief: profit-aware retention targeting

**To:** Head of Customer Retention | **From:** Retention analytics team | **Decision requested:** approve a 90-day retention pilot

**The issue.** Churn puts about **{{ value_at_risk.expected_scaled | usd }}** of 12-month customer margin at risk across the {{ scaled.base_population | num }} customers of the sample company (IBM's fictional US telco; USD). Today's rule spends the budget on the customers most likely to leave, ignoring what they are worth and what an offer costs.

## Recommendation

1. **Target by expected value, not by risk alone.** Make an offer only where churn risk x customer value x the offer's *save rate* (the share of would-be leavers it keeps) exceeds its cost. Here that means **fibre customers on month-to-month contracts with high bills**: {{ p['InternetService|Fiber optic'].list_share | pct(0) }} of the recommended list have fibre and {{ p['Contract|Month-to-month'].list_share | pct(0) }} a monthly contract, with a median bill of {{ profile.list.median_monthly_charges | usd }} against {{ profile.all.median_monthly_charges | usd }} overall. Low-risk and low-bill customers are left out.
2. **Match the offer to the segment:** {% for o in recommended_offer_mix %}{{ o.label }} ({{ o.customers | num }}){{ ", " if not loop.last else "." }}{% endfor %}

3. **Test before scaling:** one randomised pilot wave of {{ pilot.list_size_per_wave | num }} customers, half held out, and scale up only if the measured save rate clears that list's **{{ rbe | pct }} break-even**.

