- **The recommended rule is expected to add {{ headline.delta_vs_risk | usd }} for the same spend**: {{ headline.targeting_effect | usd }} from choosing customers by expected value and {{ headline.offer_design_effect | usd }} from matching offers. Valued with actual churn (at the assumed save rates), the gain is {{ headline.gain_label.label_based_simulated_net | usd }} (95% interval {{ headline.gain_label.lower_95 | usd }} to {{ headline.gain_label.upper_95 | usd }}); the part from matching offers is not proven.
- Risk-only is more precise but spends on risky customers whose value cannot repay an offer. Calling the most valuable customers {{ "loses money" if c.value.expected_net_value < 0 else "earns little" }}: risk and value are needed together.

## How much to spend

Each extra budget step returns less (figure). Keeping only steps that still return at least {{ recommendation.hurdle_marginal_roi | pct(0) }} (decided on validation data) gives about **{{ scaled.recommended_budget | usd }} for the whole base** ({{ budget.recommended_per_base_customer | usd(2) }} per customer). On the test customers this buys {{ recommended.selected_count | num }} offers for {{ recommended.cost | usd }}: expected net value {{ recommended.expected_net_value | usd }}, ROI {{ recommended.roi | pct(0) }}. Beyond {{ curve.positive_ev_share | pct(0) }} of customers nobody has positive expected value.

